### An original schedule scene: does the last-minute help displace an appointment or the original task

In this fictional example, someone asks for help at short notice. You think you can fit it in, but you already have an appointment and a deadline. Check five things before you commit:

- how much time the request actually takes;
- how long the journey takes;
- what preparation is also needed;
- which deadline has to be met first;
- whether a realistically available alternative exists.

Put the three items onto the schedule: what the added help displaces, who is affected, and which item needs someone with authority to decide. "It should be quick" is only an estimate; while it is unclear, keep it uncertain instead of promising completion.

For a new request you have not accepted, you can offer a confirmed small piece of help, suggest another time or refuse. If you find after agreeing that you cannot do it, say early what remains, the deadline and the effect, and negotiate an adjustment or a suitable replacement; the handover is complete only when the person taking it on agrees and has the necessary conditions. Sending a notice does not automatically release the original duty, and formal or high-consequence tasks still follow their own requirements. Nor is there any need to compress all your rest or hide a delay in order to keep up the appearance of composure.

The other person's urgency and what you must carry are two separate judgements. If no one is available, state clearly that the problem is not yet solved and go to whoever has authority over resources; "I will think of something" cannot replace capacity. The appointments, care and recovery time you need to keep belong in the discussion as well, and cannot be assumed cancellable.

### Not letting calm cover signals that are still unhandled

You may quickly feel a matter has passed while others are still pointing at a problem in the process, the division of work or a commitment. Break the information into five questions: does the problem still exist, who was affected and how, which item needs revising, who is actually responsible, and when the result will be confirmed. With new evidence or important consequences, check again against the necessary standard; for an ordinary matter already checked with no new information, the repeated checking can stop — constant worry is not the same as being responsible, and calm does not prove the problem is absent.

Pressure has to be understood together with the real workload, interruptions, resources and support. Try removing one adjustable burden and observe what actually changes; brief relief does not guarantee the problem is solved long term, and one word of thanks does not mean the workload has fallen. For distress that persistently affects sleep, mood or daily life, appropriate professional support can be sought, and carrying an A label is no reason to think you do not need it. The exercise here is not a treatment plan, sets no recovery speed and does not diagnose distress as a function out of balance.

